===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[drawing]] or [[diagram]] conveying information.
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=For example, while '''Figure''' 1 shows information for 516 visitor groups, '''Figure''' 3 presents data for 1,625 individuals. A note above each graph or table specifies the information illustrated. ... For example, although Joshua Tree NP visitors returned 525 questionnaires, '''Figure''' 1 shows data for only 516 respondents.|title=[[S:Joshua Tree National Park 2004 Visitor Study|Joshua Tree National Park 2004 Visitor Study]]|year=2004}}
# The representation of any form, as by drawing, painting, modelling, carving, embroidering, etc.; especially, a representation of the human body.
#: ''a '''figure''' in bronze; a '''figure''' cut in marble''
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|II|vii|passage=a coin that bears the '''figure''' of an angel}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:Nintendo Power|date=December 2007|volume=222|author=Andy Salisbury|page=34|title=Unleash the Beast|text=Ever since I was a young’n and my dad gave me a Godzilla '''figure''', I’ve been a huge fan of the big green lizard from the Land of the Rising Sun.|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/nintendo-power-222-2007/page/34/mode/1up}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Letha Ch'ien|title=This stunning de Young exhibition transforms the museum’s African galleries|journal=w:San Francisco Chronicle|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260908230256/https://www.sfchronicle.com/entertainment/arts-exhibits/article/nengi-omuku-de-young-sf-22391676.php|date=8 September 2026|passage=Omuku’s '''figures''' often seem to exist between the everyday and the dreamlike, turning ordinary moments into meditations on community, hardship and survival.}}
# A person, thing, or action representative of another.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-28|author=w:Joris Luyendijk| volume=189| issue=3| page=21| magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly| title=Our banks are out of control|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/joris-luyendijk-banking-blog/2013/jun/19/banking-britain-beyond-control| passage=Seeing the British establishment struggle with the financial sector is like watching an alcoholic&nbsp;[&hellip;].&nbsp; Until 2008 there was denial over what finance had become. When a series of bank failures made this impossible, there was widespread anger, leading to the public humiliation of symbolic '''figures'''.}}
# The appearance or impression made by the conduct or career of a person.
#: {{ux|en|He cut a sorry '''figure''' standing there in the rain.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|passage=I made some '''figure''' there.}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|passage=gentlemen of the best '''figure''' in the county}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Distinguished appearance; magnificence; conspicuous representation; splendour; show.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1729|author=w:William Law|title=A Serious Call to a Devout and Holy Life
|passage=that he may live in '''figure''' and indulgence}}
# A human figure, which dress or corset must fit to; the shape of a human body.
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=The origin of the corset is lost in remote antiquity. The '''figures''' of the early Egyptian women show clearly an artificial shape of the waist produced by some style of corset.|title=[[S:Searchlights on Health/The History, Mystery, Benefits and Injuries of the Corset|Searchlights on Health]]|year=1919|author=B. G. Jefferis; J. L. Nichols}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1966|author=James Workman|title=The Mad Emperor|publisher=Scripts|location=Melbourne, Sydney|page=53|passage=She was cunningly dressed in a black, sheer gown with gold ornaments showing her '''figure''' to perfection.}}
# A [[numeral]].
#: {{ux|en|Her income [[runs into]] six '''figures'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|amount}} A [[number]], an [[amount]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|passage=(i) in the 1966 edition of The Destruction of Dresden Irving contended that 135,000 were estimated authoritatively to have been killed and further contended that the documentation suggested a '''figure''' between 100,00 and 250,000;|title=[[S:David Irving v Penguin Books and Deborah Lipstadt/XI|David Irving v. Penguin Books and Deborah Lipstadt]]}}
# A [[shape]].
#: ''a geometrical '''figure''''', ''a plane '''figure''''', ''a solid '''figure'''''
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage=Flowers have all exquisite '''figures'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|passage=And these were not human shapes, or the shapes of anything I recognised as alive in the world, but outlines of fire that traced globes, triangles, crosses, and the luminous bodies of various geometrical '''figures'''.|title=[[S:John Silence, Physician Extraordinary/Case III|John Silence, Physician Extraordinary]]|year=1908|author=w:Algernon Blackwood}}
#* {{RQ:Bramah Max Carrados|passage=The arc of a circle may be very little, but, given that, it is possible to construct the entire '''figure'''.}}
# A visible pattern as in wood or cloth.
#: {{ux|en|The muslin was of a pretty '''figure'''.}}
# Any complex dance move<sup>{{w|Dance move|W}}</sup>.
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5|passage=Although the Celebrity was almost impervious to sarcasm, he was now beginning to exhibit visible signs of uneasiness,{{...}}. It was with a palpable relief that he heard the first warning notes of the '''figure'''.}}
# A [[figure of speech]].
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=20|passage=to represent the imagination under the '''figure''' of a wing}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} The form of a [[syllogism]] with respect to the relative position of the middle [[term]].
# {{lb|en|astrology}} A [[horoscope]]; the [[diagram]] of the [[aspect]]s of the [[astrological]] [[house]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1889|author=Franz Hartmann|title=The Principles of Astrological Geomancy
|passage=its quality, like those of all the rest, is determined by its position in the house of the astrological '''figure'''}}
# {{lb|en|music}} Any short succession of notes, either as melody or as a group of chords, which produce a single complete and distinct impression.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1888|author=w:George Grove|title=Beethoven's Nine Symphonies: Analytical Essays
|passage=Here, Beethoven limits the syncopations and modifications of rhythm which are so prominent in the first and third movements, and employs a rapid, busy, and most melodious '''figure''' in the Violins, which is irresistible in its gay and brilliant effect{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A form of melody or accompaniment kept up through a strain or passage; a [[motif]]; a florid embellishment.

